<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=bakelite">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Tools and materials</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bakelite</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1907">1907</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bakelite is a hard phenol formaldehyde resin, the first plastic made from synthetic components, developed by the chemist Leo Baekeland in Yonkers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, in 1907 and patented in 1909. Because it could be moulded into any shape and is heat-resistant and electrically non-conductive, it was used for electrical insulators, radio and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telephone">telephone</a> casings, kitchenware, jewellery and toys. Its commercial success led to other synthetic plastics, and the American Chemical Society named it a National Historic Chemical Landmark as the world's first commercial synthetic plastic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bakelite.jpg" alt="Bakelite" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Robneild at English Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bakelite_radio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
